WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.951
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.951 --> 00:00:02.173
Tuesday the tenth.

00:00:02.173 --> 00:00:06.655
A bright, blustery one, with a short calendar and an open evening.

00:00:06.655 --> 00:00:09.167
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:13.989
Why are those hard intervals four minutes long, and not thirty seconds?

00:00:13.989 --> 00:00:15.347
Answer's at the end.

00:00:15.347 --> 00:00:17.248
Weather's mostly about wind.

00:00:17.248 --> 00:00:24.718
Sun and high clouds, low sixties, with northwest gusts in the twenties and maybe a quick shower around midday.

00:00:24.718 --> 00:00:27.706
That's enough to push back on the ride home.

00:00:27.706 --> 00:00:32.120
Rain moves in Thursday afternoon, and it turns chilly after that.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.071
Okay, the day.

00:00:33.071 --> 00:00:37.077
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:37.077 --> 00:00:38.978
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:38.978 --> 00:00:40.880
Nothing on there after that.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:48.010
And that group singing night on Scott Avenue from Saturday's memo is tonight at seven, if you're curious.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:48.961
Alright, news.

00:00:48.961 --> 00:00:50.319
One from your world.

00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:53.035
Adobe just became Disney's official A.I.

00:00:53.035 --> 00:00:57.449
partner, announced as Adobe's big MAX conference opened in Miami.

00:00:57.449 --> 00:01:07.771
Adobe will build custom image and video models trained only on Disney's own characters and worlds, for the studio, ESPN, and the parks and cruise lines.

00:01:07.771 --> 00:01:09.537
That's the part to notice.

00:01:09.537 --> 00:01:14.222
Big brands don't want a general model that's seen the whole internet.

00:01:14.222 --> 00:01:17.074
They want one that only knows their stuff.

00:01:17.074 --> 00:01:22.778
Concept artists are wary, though, since it lands while Disney is cutting costs hard.

00:01:22.778 --> 00:01:24.544
A strange one from France.

00:01:24.544 --> 00:01:35.952
Officials there say a hacking unit from Iran's Revolutionary Guards broke into the refrigeration systems at nearly seven hundred supermarkets and food sites in October.

00:01:35.952 --> 00:01:38.193
At least fifteen fires broke out.

00:01:38.193 --> 00:01:44.033
Similar break-ins hit more than a hundred places across Europe, including blood banks.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:48.243
France wants European sanctions, and Iran hasn't answered yet.

00:01:48.243 --> 00:01:50.009
And a surprise on climate.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:57.275
The yearly global carbon count projects fossil fuel emissions will fall this year, by about half a percent.

00:01:57.275 --> 00:02:00.127
That's the first drop since twenty twenty.

00:02:00.127 --> 00:02:02.300
The reason isn't policy, though.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:07.529
It's the oil price spike from the Iran war, which pushed people to burn less.

00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:07.801
U.S.

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:10.992
emissions are down a bit more than one percent.

00:02:10.992 --> 00:02:15.814
The scientists warn it could flip right back once oil gets cheap again.

00:02:15.814 --> 00:02:17.851
Okay, here's one for Saturday.

00:02:17.851 --> 00:02:27.697
The Sultan Room, the little venue on the Bushwick and East Williamsburg line, has AJ Lee and Blue Summit at seven, with The Cody Sisters opening.

00:02:27.697 --> 00:02:33.945
It's a California bluegrass band, tight harmonies and fast picking, about twenty-five bucks.

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:38.562
It's a small room, so you're close enough to watch hands on strings.

00:02:38.562 --> 00:02:41.211
A warm thing to do on a chilly weekend.

00:02:41.211 --> 00:02:43.452
Today's challenge is a photo one.

00:02:43.452 --> 00:02:45.625
The wind's doing the work today.

00:02:45.625 --> 00:02:55.675
Find one thing it's moving, a flag, a tree, a loose sign, someone's scarf, and make one photo where anybody could tell it's windy without a caption.

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:57.305
Try it a couple of ways.

00:02:57.305 --> 00:03:00.700
Freeze it mid-whip, or hold still and let it blur.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.669
One frame you like is enough.

00:03:02.669 --> 00:03:05.182
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:05.182 --> 00:03:09.664
Remember finding out the voice service only hands back raw P.C.M.?

00:03:09.664 --> 00:03:12.516
Yesterday was about the dots in that file.

00:03:12.516 --> 00:03:18.967
Today's about the two numbers that decide how many dots there are, and why that file is so big.

00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:23.177
The first number is the sample rate, how many dots per second.

00:03:23.177 --> 00:03:28.406
The rule is that you need at least two dots for every wave you want to catch.

00:03:28.406 --> 00:03:32.752
So to capture a sound up to some pitch, you sample at double it.

00:03:32.752 --> 00:03:41.172
People hear up to about twenty thousand cycles a second, which is why music uses about forty-four thousand samples a second.

00:03:41.172 --> 00:03:43.210
Speech doesn't need that much.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:51.698
The useful stuff in a voice sits lower, so a lot of voice services, including the one reading this, use twenty-four thousand.

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:58.149
That still catches everything up to twelve thousand, which covers the crispness of an s or a t.

00:03:58.149 --> 00:04:03.106
The second number is bit depth, how finely each dot's height is measured.

00:04:03.106 --> 00:04:07.452
Sixteen bits gives you about sixty-five thousand possible steps.

00:04:07.452 --> 00:04:13.360
That's fine enough that the rounding errors sit way below anything you'd hear on a run.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:14.243
Now multiply.

00:04:14.243 --> 00:04:21.102
Twenty-four thousand dots a second, two bytes each, is about forty-eight thousand bytes every second.

00:04:21.102 --> 00:04:25.923
A five minute episode comes out around fourteen megabytes of raw audio.

00:04:25.923 --> 00:04:31.491
Turn that into an M.P.3 at a normal voice setting and it's closer to two or three.

00:04:31.491 --> 00:04:39.572
Same five minutes, about a sixth of the size, because the M.P.3 stores a description of the sound instead of every dot.

00:04:39.572 --> 00:04:43.035
That's why the pipeline converts before publishing.

00:04:43.035 --> 00:04:43.714
Raw P.C.M.

00:04:43.714 --> 00:04:46.227
is perfect for editing and measuring.

00:04:46.227 --> 00:04:48.536
M.P.3 is what you send to a phone.

00:04:48.536 --> 00:04:53.493
So: calendar says run at seven and Godinger till five, with a free night.

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:54.512
Shoot the wind.

00:04:54.512 --> 00:05:01.234
The answer: your heart takes a minute or two to climb, so four minutes buys real time near the top.

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.000
Have a good Tuesday, Ziad.
